08},{"cell_type":"In","gene_symbol":"CRYAB","gene_symbol_current":"CRYAB","gene_id":"ENSG00000109846","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":0.452441,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.226851,"nucleus_score":-1.60606,"pct_ad":0.228483,"pct_control":0.239963,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999889,"donor_log2fc":-0.0388392,"donor_lfcse":0.258537,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-6.03406e-07},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"CRYAB","gene_symbol_current":"CRYAB","gene_id":"ENSG00000109846","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":5.75132e-07,"nucleus_log2fc":0.245895,"nucleus_score":5.41205,"pct_ad":0.598018,"pct_control":0.546757,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.958934,"donor_log2fc":0.0745298,"donor_lfcse":0.235572,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00130522}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000109846","module":"greenyellow","kme":0.320938,"hub":true,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.56571,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_id":"ENSG00000109846","module":"turquoise","kme":0.297993,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.572565,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000109846","module":"turquoise","kme":0.315411,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.446504,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_id":"ENSG00000109846","module":"turquoise","kme":0.515244,"hub":true,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.415372,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000109846","entrez_id":["1410"],"uniprot":["P02511"],"ncbi_summary":"Mammalian lens crystallins are divided into alpha, beta, and gamma families. Alpha crystallins are composed of two gene products: alpha-A and alpha-B, for acidic and basic, respectively. Alpha crystallins can be induced by heat shock and are members of the small heat shock protein (HSP20) family. They act as molecular chaperones although they do not renature proteins and release them in the fashion of a true chaperone; instead they hold them in large soluble aggregates. These heterogeneous aggregates consist of 30-40 subunits; the alpha-A and alpha-B subunits have a 3:1 ratio, respectively. Two additional functions of alpha crystallins are an autokinase activity and participation in the intracellular architecture. The encoded protein has been identified as a moonlighting protein based on its ability to perform mechanistically distinct functions. Alpha-A and alpha-B gene products are differentially expressed; alpha-A is preferentially restricted to the lens and alpha-B is expressed widely in many tissues and organs. Elevated expression of alpha-B crystallin occurs in many neurological diseases; a missense mutation cosegregated in a family with a desmin-related myopathy. Alternative splicing results in multiple transcript variants. [provided by RefSeq, Jan 2019].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P02511","text":"May contribute to the transparency and refractive index of the lens. Has chaperone-like activity, preventing aggregation of various proteins under a wide range of stress conditions. In lens epithelial cells, stabilizes the ATP6V1A protein, preventing its degradation by the proteasome (By similarity)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_03","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}